cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use Causal Integrity, please cite it as below."
title: "Causal Integrity: A Research Hypothesis for AI Alignment"
type: software
authors:
  - name: "NetworkYum"
    website: "https://github.com/NetworkYum"
repository-code: "https://github.com/NetworkYum/causal-integrity"
url: "https://github.com/NetworkYum/causal-integrity"
abstract: >-
  Research hypothesis for AI alignment: whether the causal commitments implicit
  in purposeful action can ground consistent restraints against causing harm.
  Purposeful action relies on causal regularity, and actual causal contributions
  cannot be erased by preference or assertion. The proposed ethical extension is
  that an agent cannot coherently rely on agency, preferences, and causal
  attribution for its own pursuits while selectively discarding those same
  relationships when its actions affect others. The primary conceptual reference
  (docs/CONCEPTS.md) develops Universally Preferable Behaviour (UPB) as
  universalization over action types rather than momentary preferences, together
  with demonstrated preference and trust collapse (Section 5). Self-collapse via
  internal decoherence is an additional stability hypothesis, not the foundation.
  Specified in First-Order Logic with a Lean 4 Lake package (exploratory axioms
  and derived lemmas; `lake build`). Status: public research hypothesis with S1
  hygiene; the full ethical argument is not machine-proven (see docs/CONCEPTS.md
  and docs/definition-of-done.md).
keywords:
